      "math_academy_id": "q-213992",
      "sequence_position": 16,
      "problem": "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $Z$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}},\nbut \n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nFor our relation, notice that\n\n- $(3,5)\\in R$ since $3∤5,$ and\n\n- $(5,6)\\in R$ since $5∤6,$ but\n\n- $(3,6)\\notin R$ since $3∣6.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(3,5)\\in R$ and $(5,6)\\in R$ but \n$(3,6)\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$",
